<!-- Bio-story narration source.
     Voice: Polly Matthew / Piper en_US-ryan-high.
     Persona: Daniel M., Grade 11 Biology student. -->

<!-- slide-01.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Inside a Grade eleven Biology lesson.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Photosynthesis — the engine that turns sunlight into food and oxygen.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Watch what Daniel sees on his laptop tonight.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-02.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Meet Daniel — Grade eleven Science, Biology stream.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Tonight he's working through photosynthesis.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Lesson, quiz, tutorial — about twenty-five minutes of focused study.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-03.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    He opens his dashboard.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    Continue where you left off — photosynthesis is queued.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    One click. The lesson opens.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-04.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    The lesson page opens.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Title, the first section, the big-picture equation —
    pitched two grades below his own so it never feels like a wall of jargon.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-05.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Stage one — the light reactions.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Inside the thylakoid membranes, chlorophyll absorbs light energy.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    Water splits. Oxygen leaves. ATP and NADPH are made.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-06.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Stage two — the Calvin cycle.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Out in the stroma, Ru-BIS-co grabs CO2 from the air.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    The energy from stage one fuels three turns of the cycle —
    yielding one glucose molecule.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-07.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Why are plants green?
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue strongly — those are the high-energy peaks.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    But it reflects green.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    Green is what reaches our eyes.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-08.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Quiz time.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Eight questions. Daniel hovers on red briefly, then lands on green —
    that's the colour chlorophyll reflects.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-09.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Correct.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    The explanation appears below — chlorophyll reflects green,
    which is what reaches our eyes.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Two short sentences. No red X.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-10.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    A tutorial section opens.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Photon hits chlorophyll.
    <break time="200ms"/>
    Electron flows down the chain.
    <break time="200ms"/>
    Calvin cycle uses the energy to build glucose.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-11.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    He finishes the unit.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Eleven nights of biology in a row.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    The streak counter knows — and so does the encouragement nudge,
    but only when it's earned.
  </prosody>
</speak>

